WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:08.680
Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra is the author of one of the world's most famous novels, Don Quixote de la Mancha

2
00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:18.403
. This is the story of a country gentleman who decides to become a knight after reading many romantic tales about knights in armor and highborn

3
00:00:18.404 --> 00:00:29.327
ladies. This gentleman takes the name Don Quixote, and he and his down-to-earth squire, Sancho Panza, go on quests to find adventure and fight

4
00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:32.776
injustice. This simple story changed literature

5
00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:42.594
forever. Cervantes was born in 1547 outside of Madrid, Spain, in the city of Alcalá de Henares. He was one of seven children.

6
00:00:42.595 --> 00:00:49.094
Not much is known of his early childhood, but one thing is certain—he was an eager reader of books.

7
00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:55.101
In the fall of 1569, young Cervantes went to Rome to work for the Catholic Church.

8
00:00:55.102 --> 00:01:01.313
Some claim he really went to Rome to escape arrest for having wounded another man in a duel.

9
00:01:01.314 --> 00:01:14.756
Whatever the reason, being in Rome allowed him to study art, philosophy, and literature and to experience the many changes happening in society during the transition from medieval times to the early Renaissance.

10
00:01:14.757 --> 00:01:19.953
Scientific thinking was on the rise and traditional knowledge was being challenged.

11
00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:27.342
Cosmopolitan areas, where people with different beliefs and experiences tended to come together, were expanding.

12
00:01:27.343 --> 00:01:32.609
In 1570, when he was 24, Cervantes joined the Spanish army.

13
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:43.104
At that time, the Muslim Turks of the Ottoman Empire were fighting European Christians for control of the Mediterranean, and Cervantes wanted to be a part of the fight.

14
00:01:43.105 --> 00:01:54.877
He joined the Holy League, a mighty armada of three hundred ships and the Catholic armies of Pope Pius V, Venice, and Spain, which totaled about eighty thousand men. In

15
00:01:54.878 --> 00:02:02.703
1571, Cervantes was on a ship near Greece when he was shot three times in the Battle of Lepanto. One

16
00:02:02.704 --> 00:02:09.309
bullet hit him in the left hand, crippling it. He was never able to use that hand again. The

17
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:18.301
battle was a huge victory for the Holy League, and Cervantes later wrote, "On that day, Ottoman pride and arrogance were broken." Cervantes

18
00:02:18.302 --> 00:02:24.918
served for four more years, fighting in several more military campaigns against the Turks. He

19
00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:35.111
was promoted and was now receiving a high salary; nevertheless, tired of war, Cervantes decided to return to Spain in 1575. That

20
00:02:35.112 --> 00:02:40.069
September, he boarded a galley named El Sol in Naples, Italy. The

21
00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:48.387
ship was attacked by Turkish pirates, and Cervantes and the other survivors of the attack were taken to Algiers as captives. A

22
00:02:48.388 --> 00:02:58.926
city on the coast of North Africa, Algiers was the center of Turkish piracy, where supplies, stolen merchandise, and slaves were freely bought and sold. Prisoners

23
00:02:58.927 --> 00:03:07.583
were treated like slaves, forced to work all day and night with little food, water, or rest. Many died in captivity. If a

24
00:03:07.584 --> 00:03:12.861
slave was wealthy, pirates would attempt to get a ransom from the person’s family. Because Cervantes

25
00:03:12.862 --> 00:03:21.735
had letters of recommendation from important people in the Spanish army, the Turks thought he must be a valuable captive. Even though

26
00:03:21.736 --> 00:03:26.791
Cervantes told them he had no money, the Turks did not believe him. They decided

27
00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:33.791
to ransom him for five hundred gold coins, a sum his family could hardly hope to raise. Cervantes made

28
00:03:33.792 --> 00:03:38.621
four escape attempts but was captured every time. After five

29
00:03:38.622 --> 00:03:44.843
years in captivity, Cervantes' ransom was finally paid by a group of friars. Cervantes returned

30
00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:53.947
home to Spain in 1580, but his long imprisonment had left an enduring mark on him as a man and as a writer. As soon

31
00:03:53.948 --> 00:03:58.795
as he reached home, Cervantes started his writing career in earnest. In 1585,

32
00:03:58.796 --> 00:04:06.168
a few months after getting married, Cervantes published the romantic novel La Galatea . Some

33
00:04:06.169 --> 00:04:14.827
of his plays were being performed in theaters in Madrid as well, but they did not provide Cervantes with much money to take care of his household. So,

34
00:04:14.828 --> 00:04:27.791
in 1587, Cervantes accepted a government position requisitioning grain and other supplies for the Spanish Armada, a fleet of over one hundred ships being prepared to battle England. After the

35
00:04:27.792 --> 00:04:42.287
armada was destroyed in 1588 by Elizabeth I and her navy—a horrible embarrassment for Spain—Cervantes became a tax collector, going from town to town and collecting rents and taxes for the government. Suspicion that

36
00:04:42.288 --> 00:04:53.275
he was falsifying his accounts got him in trouble with the government, and he wound up in jail twice over the course of six years for financial impropriety. Many scholars,

37
00:04:53.276 --> 00:04:58.942
however, believe that he was honest but simply a bad businessman. Cervantes later

38
00:04:58.943 --> 00:05:04.823
said that part of his masterpiece Don Quixote was written in prison. In 1605,

39
00:05:04.824 --> 00:05:11.211
at the age of 58, Cervantes published the first part of Don Quixote . The

40
00:05:11.212 --> 00:05:19.043
novel was a huge success, one he badly needed after years of intense hardship and financial insecurity. Ten

41
00:05:19.044 --> 00:05:25.209
years later, he published the second part, which some critics think surpasses the first. Cervantes

42
00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:36.307
had lost much of his idealism about the Spanish Empire and the romance of battle, and in Don Quixote , he makes fun of everyone—nobility and commoners. His

43
00:05:36.308 --> 00:05:45.301
main character, Don Quixote, bases his life's purpose on upholding the "truth" that he has read about in romantic stories. Don

44
00:05:45.302 --> 00:05:54.286
Quixote does battle with windmills, thinking they are giants, and mistakes a farm girl for a wellborn lady needing his protection. When

45
00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:59.611
he comes to a small inn, the knight thinks it is the castle of a great lord. Sancho

46
00:05:59.612 --> 00:06:09.549
Panza constantly tries to get his master to see things as they really are, but Don Quixote insists on seeing the world in magical ways. Readers

47
00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:16.715
everywhere were touched by both the humor and the sadness of Cervantes' depiction of the human experience. The

48
00:06:16.716 --> 00:06:22.053
story is a comedy that makes fun of idealistic and impractical people. But

49
00:06:22.054 --> 00:06:28.871
at the same time, it is a tragedy about a troubled man struggling with self-delusion and social change. It

50
00:06:28.872 --> 00:06:34.813
remains up to the reader to decide whether Don Quixote is a hero or a fool. Cervantes

51
00:06:34.814 --> 00:06:42.409
wrote many poems, novels, short stories, and plays, but none became as famous as Don Quixote .

52
00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:48.636
The book about the wayward knight is thought by many scholars to be the basis of the modern novel. Before

53
00:06:48.637 --> 00:06:54.184
Cervantes, most stories were written to convey unchanging moral "truths." Cervantes

54
00:06:54.185 --> 00:07:06.345
broke that tradition and created a completely new type of literature, which questions what is real and what is not through characters who are themselves undecided about reality. Not

55
00:07:06.346 --> 00:07:12.663
only did Cervantes help invent a new kind of story, but he also inspired a new word. We

56
00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:21.000
use this word today to sum up a person or plan that is idealistic, impulsive, and romantic: quixotic .

